{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_1",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_36",
      "quote": "This is a living systematic review of clinical study registrations (clinical trials and otherwise) for the diagnosis, treatment and prevention of COVID-19, an important endeavour in the midst of the pandemic and a concurrent infodemic with rapid evidence proliferation.",
      "reason": "The manuscript text explicitly describes the work as a \"living systematic review\", which establishes that the report is a systematic review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The primary objective of this living systematic review is to provide an open, accessible and frequently updated resource summarising the characteristics of COVID-19 clinical study registrations. Herein, we present the results of the baseline review of all eligible trial records identified by the systematic searches as of 3 April 2020 and initial synthesis of clinical study characteristics.",
      "reason": "The text states the review's primary objective and scope (summarising characteristics of COVID-19 clinical study registrations), but the document title itself (\"Baseline results of a living systematic review for COVID-19\") only indicates the disease focus and living/systematic-review status; it does not explicitly provide the full main objective or specific population/intervention details in the title, so the requirement is only partially met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_36",
      "quote": "This is a living systematic review of clinical study registrations (clinical trials and otherwise) for the diagnosis, treatment and prevention of COVID-19,",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly uses the phrase \"living systematic review,\" and the document title likewise includes \"living systematic review,\" providing the supplementary information (living-review status) requested."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "The urgent need for immediate solutions to tackle public health emergencies of international concern undermines effective coordination and collaboration between researchers 1,2 . Since the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) outbreak was reported in December 2019, a huge number of clinical studies have been initiated, firstly in China, South Korea, then subsequently in Europe, Japan, North America and Australia. The number of trials recorded in clinical trial registries is proliferating exponentially, with many independent research efforts designed to answer similar questions. Whilst multicentred trials can potentially improve generalisability of study results across widely divergent endemic settings, this requires comparable study designs and comparators. Conversely, independent trials with small sample sizes and underrepresentation of some patient populations limit the acquisition of substantive evidence to assess the effects and applicability of the many diagnostic methods, therapeutic and prophylactic strategies targeted to address the COVID-19 pandemic 3 .",
      "reason": "The introduction explicitly describes the current state of knowledge (rapid proliferation of trial registrations, geographic spread) and key uncertainties/limitations (many small, independent trials, underrepresentation of populations, heterogeneity), satisfying the requirement to describe current knowledge and uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background: Since the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) outbreak was first reported in December 2019, many independent trials have been planned that aim to answer similar questions. Tools allowing researchers to review studies already underway can facilitate collaboration, cooperation and harmonisation. The Infectious Diseases Data Observatory (IDDO) has undertaken a living systematic review (LSR) to provide an open, accessible and frequently updated resource summarising characteristics of COVID-19 study registrations.",
      "reason": "The abstract and manuscript state the importance of providing an open, frequently updated resource to summarise study registrations and explain how such a review facilitates collaboration, harmonisation and avoidance of duplication, which explains the need for conducting the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No explicit statement was found in the provided evidence explaining the need for the current review in the context of existing similar systematic reviews, nor citation/discussion of prior reviews or justification as an update/replication."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This conditional item applies to reviews of intervention effects. The provided evidence describes a living review of clinical trial registrations and does not present assessment of intervention effects or propose mechanisms of action; thus the requirement does not apply to this manuscript based on the available text."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The conditional item concerns inclusion of a logic model for complex interventions or delivery contexts. The supplied evidence does not present or propose a logic model and the review focuses on trial registrations rather than complex intervention delivery frameworks; therefore this requirement is not applicable based on the available text."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
